    "q-325143": {
      "dom_id": "step-q325143",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "What is the remainder of $-4{x}^{3}+16$ divided by $2{x}^{2}-3x+5?$\n\nThe remainder is\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the dividend is missing the ${x}^{2}$ and $x$ terms. So, we first rewrite it by including the missing terms with zero coefficients, as follows:\n\n$$\n-4{x}^{3}+16=-4{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+0x+16\n$$\n\nNow, using the method of polynomial division, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-2x-3 \\\\  & 2{x}^{2}-3x+5\\,\\enclose{longdiv}{\\,\\,-4{x}^{3}+0{x}^{2}+\\phantom{0}0x+6\\,\\,} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-10x}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,-6{x}^{2}+10x+16 \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{-\\,6{x}^{2}+9x-15}_{―} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,x+31\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n- the quotient is $-2x-3,$ and\n\n- the remainder is $x+31.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
